% ============================================================================
% triangle-types — the three types of triangle CLASSIFIED BY THEIR SIDES and
% the defining side-condition each one is built on (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first classifications a geometry student learns, and a rung a
% GEOMETRY agent reasons UP from: classify a triangle by counting how many of
% its three sides are equal. "An equilateral triangle has all three sides
% equal; an isosceles triangle has two equal sides; a scalene triangle has three
% unequal sides." Before you can reason about a triangle's angles, symmetry, or
% congruence you must first know WHICH of these three side-classes it belongs to.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? triangle_sides(isosceles, $Condition)      % two_equal_sides
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `triangle_sides(triangle, condition)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% side-condition WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on a word that is not one of these three side-classes (it never invents a
% condition).
%
% This table classifies triangles BY THEIR SIDES. It is DISTINCT from a
% classification by ANGLES (acute / right / obtuse), which lives in a separate
% library — a scalene triangle can be acute, right, or obtuse, so the two
% classifications are independent axes.
%
% Each of the three side-classes and the defining side-condition the source
% states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     triangle type   defining side-condition   a beginner's cue
%     -------------   -----------------------   --------------------------------
%     equilateral     three_equal_sides         all three sides the same length
%     isosceles       two_equal_sides           exactly two sides the same length
%     scalene         three_unequal_sides       all three sides different lengths
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a side-condition and recall its type:
%
%     ? triangle_sides($Type, three_equal_sides)   % equilateral
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three side-classes — `right`, `acute`,
% `square` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a side-classification recall ABSTAINS
% on it rather than inventing a condition. That honest-abstention property is
% what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% type→condition pairs it can ground, and only those. (`right` and `acute` are
% ANGLE classes, not side classes; `square` is not a triangle at all.)
%
% The `condition` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be
% the defining side-condition the source EXPLICITLY states for that type — never
% one the source does not support. (Where the everyday word differs from the
% source's word — the source says "three unequal sides" for scalene, not "no
% equal sides" — the atom uses the source's own word, so every value stays
% literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every type→condition pair
% below was read out of a fetched Wolfram MathWorld page, and any pair whose
% condition could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair,
% with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states
% it:
%
%   equilateral → three_equal_sides
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EquilateralTriangle.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — equilateral triangle)
%     "An equilateral triangle is a triangle with all three sides of equal
%      length a, corresponding to what could also be known as a 'regular'
%      triangle."
%
%   isosceles → two_equal_sides
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/IsoscelesTriangle.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — isosceles triangle)
%     "An isosceles triangle is a triangle with (at least) two equal sides."
%
%   scalene → three_unequal_sides
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ScaleneTriangle.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — scalene triangle)
%     "A scalene triangle is a triangle that has three unequal sides."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the MathWorld statement that
% fixes the first row (equilateral → three_equal_sides). Every OTHER row's
% per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each condition remains
% independently checkable. All sources are Wolfram MathWorld, a widely-cited
% mathematics reference, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table triangle_sides {
    columns triangle, condition

    row (equilateral, three_equal_sides)
    row (isosceles, two_equal_sides)
    row (scalene, three_unequal_sides)

    source "An equilateral triangle is a triangle with all three sides of equal length a, corresponding to what could also be known as a 'regular' triangle."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EquilateralTriangle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
